## 2. CORE CONCEPTS

### Image
- Blueprint/template for creating containers
- Read-only file system
- Contains: OS, runtime, libraries, code, environment variables
- Stored in registries (Docker Hub, ECR, GCR)

```bash
# Image is like a class
docker image ls
docker pull ubuntu:20.04
docker build -t my-app:1.0 .
```

### Container
- Running instance of an image
- Writable layer on top of image
- Isolated process environment
- Can be started, stopped, deleted

```bash
# Container is like an object/instance
docker run -d --name my-container my-app:1.0
docker ps
docker logs my-container
```

### Registry
- Repository for storing images
- Public: Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry
- Private: AWS ECR, Azure ACR, Artifactory

### Layer
- Docker images are built in layers
- Each instruction in Dockerfile creates a layer
- Layers are cached for faster rebuilds
- Only changed layers are rebuilt

```
Dockerfile:
FROM ubuntu:20.04          # Layer 1: Base image
RUN apt-get update         # Layer 2: System updates
RUN apt-get install python # Layer 3: Python installation
COPY app.py /app/          # Layer 4: Copy application
CMD ["python", "app.py"]   # Layer 5: Default command
```

### Volumes
- Persistent data storage
- Survive container deletion
- Can be shared between containers
- Three types: Named volumes, Bind mounts, tmpfs

```bash
# Named volume
docker run -v my-data:/data my-app

# Bind mount
docker run -v /host/path:/container/path my-app

# tmpfs mount (in-memory)
docker run --tmpfs /tmp my-app
```

### Networks
- Enable container-to-container communication
- Bridge network (default, isolated)
- Host network (shares host network)
- Overlay network (for swarm/Kubernetes)

```bash
docker network create my-network
docker run --network my-network --name service1 my-app
docker run --network my-network --name service2 my-app
# service1 can communicate with service2 using hostname
```

